<section id="consolidated-legacy-evidence" class="legacy-consolidations"><h2>Consolidated software comparisons: retained source evidence</h2><p>The original pairwise articles contained details worth retaining: platform and app restrictions, module-level boundaries, ownership costs and the difference between documented manufacturer claims and verified vehicle support. Those public URLs now redirect to this consolidated source, while their original source files and SVG assets remain available in the Git history for traceability.</p><article id="bimmercode-vs-carly" class="legacy-case"><h3>BimmerCode vs Carly: Which BMW/MINI Tool Fits Your Use Case?</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Consolidated from the earlier `/guides/bimmercode-vs-carly/` article, now redirected here. Core decision evidence and original diagrams are integrated below.</p><h4>BimmerCode vs Carly product scope</h4><p>Official Carly app dashboard press image. The screen illustrates the product interface, not guaranteed feature availability for every vehicle. Copyright Carly Solutions GmbH &amp;amp; Co. KG — official press asset.</p>
<h4>Adapter compatibility: OBD fit is not enough</h4><p>Check three things before purchasing: the adapter must be listed for the app, listed for the exact vehicle/series, and supported on the phone/platform being used. A connector that fits the OBD port is not proof of app compatibility.</p>
<h4>BMW and MINI generation compatibility</h4><p>BimmerCode's FAQ broadly says it supports nearly all BMW and MINI vehicles built in or after 2005, but its app-store model rows and adapter cutoffs are more granular. Use the supported-vehicle checker rather than turning the broad cutoff into a guarantee.</p><details><summary>Original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-carly/v01-product-scope-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-carly/v01-product-scope.svg" alt="BimmerCode vs Carly: Which BMW/MINI Tool Fits Your Use Case?: preserved source diagram 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 1; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-carly/v02-configuration-intersection-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-carly/v02-configuration-intersection.svg" alt="BimmerCode vs Carly: Which BMW/MINI Tool Fits Your Use Case?: preserved source diagram 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 2; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article id="bimmercode-vs-foxwell-nt530" class="legacy-case"><h3>BimmerCode vs Foxwell NT530: Coding App or Diagnostic Handheld?</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Consolidated from the earlier `/guides/bimmercode-vs-foxwell-nt530/` article, now redirected here. Core decision evidence and original diagrams are integrated below.</p><h4>Coding and diagnostics are different jobs</h4><p>BimmerCode owns coding while NT530 is a diagnostic and service handheld; overlap does not erase the category boundary.</p>
<h4>Write the job before choosing the tool</h4><p>BimmerCode and the NT530 are not direct substitutes until the required job is written down. Changing a documented coding option, reading a fault, running a named service function and programming a module are different categories with different evidence and risk.</p>
<h4>The complete BimmerCode working chain</h4><p>BimmerCode uses a supported phone, a supported OBD adapter and the app to change documented coding options on supported control units. The buyer must confirm the vehicle and adapter combination in the current selector; the app name alone does not approve every BMW generation.</p><details><summary>Original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-foxwell-nt530/cs044-visual-a-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-foxwell-nt530/cs044-visual-a.svg" alt="BimmerCode vs Foxwell NT530: Coding App or Diagnostic Handheld?: preserved source diagram 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 1; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-foxwell-nt530/cs044-visual-b-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-foxwell-nt530/cs044-visual-b.svg" alt="BimmerCode vs Foxwell NT530: Coding App or Diagnostic Handheld?: preserved source diagram 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 2; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article id="bimmercode-vs-protool" class="legacy-case"><h3>BimmerCode vs ProTool: Coding App or BMW Toolset?</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Consolidated from the earlier `/guides/bimmercode-vs-protool/` article, now redirected here. Core decision evidence and original diagrams are integrated below.</p><h4>Platform can settle the decision early</h4><p>ProTool's official page identifies Android as its platform. If the maintained device is iOS-only, that is a hard boundary rather than a preference. BimmerCode supports both iOS and Android, but its FAQ explains that purchases do not automatically move between the platform stores.</p>
<h4>Cost: price the required scope, not the biggest bundle</h4><p>At verification, ProTool publicly listed Diagnostic and Coding licenses separately at $99.99 each and the combined Master license at $174.99. Those figures are date-sensitive and belong to the official storefront, not to a promise about future pricing or tax.</p>
<h4>Coverage has three dimensions</h4><p>“Supports my BMW” can mean at least three things: the chassis is generally listed, the relevant control unit exists in that car, and the exact option or function is supported. A model badge or production series only answers part of the first question.</p><details><summary>Original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-protool/cs026-scope-orbits-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-protool/cs026-scope-orbits.svg" alt="BimmerCode vs ProTool: Coding App or BMW Toolset?: preserved source diagram 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 1; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-protool/cs026-choice-sequence-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/bimmercode-vs-protool/cs026-choice-sequence.svg" alt="BimmerCode vs ProTool: Coding App or BMW Toolset?: preserved source diagram 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 2; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article id="bimmerlink-vs-bimmer-tool" class="legacy-case"><h3>BimmerLink vs bimmer-tool: Choose by Platform, Engine, and Service Job</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Consolidated from the earlier `/guides/bimmerlink-vs-bimmer-tool/` article, now redirected here. Core decision evidence and original diagrams are integrated below.</p><h4>Match the platform and adapter before anything else</h4><p>If you use an iPhone, BimmerLink is the natural fit because it ships for iOS as well as Android; bimmer-tool's presence is centered on Android. On Android, both are in play, so the adapter becomes the next filter.</p>
<h4>Weigh the diesel and DPF functions carefully</h4><p>This is where the apps genuinely differ. BimmerLink lets you check DPF state — soot and ash load, time since the last regeneration — and start a regeneration. bimmer-tool covers regeneration too, and adds detailed DPF data and the ability to reset DPF adaptation values, which some diesel workflows call for after service.</p>
<h4>Line up service functions and pricing</h4><p>Both apps register a new battery and reset oil-service intervals. BimmerLink additionally offers electronic parking brake service mode and service resets tied to jobs like brake-pad replacement. Match the specific routine you need — not the size of the menu — to your exact chassis and software version, because a function present on one model may be absent or behave differently on another.</p><details><summary>Original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><img src="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-bimmer-tool/cs-082-usecase-matrix-desktop.svg" alt="BimmerLink vs bimmer-tool: Choose by Platform, Engine, and Service Job: preserved source diagram 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 1; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><img src="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-bimmer-tool/cs-082-decision-path-desktop.svg" alt="BimmerLink vs bimmer-tool: Choose by Platform, Engine, and Service Job: preserved source diagram 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 2; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article id="bimmerlink-vs-carly" class="legacy-case"><h3>BimmerLink vs Carly: Which BMW Tool Fits Your Actual Job?</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Consolidated from the earlier `/guides/bimmerlink-vs-carly/` article, now redirected here. Core decision evidence and original diagrams are integrated below.</p><h4>The fast decision</h4><p>Choose BimmerLink when your priority is BMW-focused fault access, live sensor work, data logging, battery registration, parking-brake service mode, service reset, or another function explicitly listed for your car. Choose Carly when you want its wider mix of diagnostics, coding, maintenance guidance, and multi-brand options, and you accept the current paid-plan model.</p>
<h4>Adapter fit can reverse the decision</h4><p>Both paths rely on more than an app name. Record the BMW model, chassis, build year, phone operating system, and the exact adapter model before buying. A generic Bluetooth OBD dongle may fit the socket while using the wrong radio method or lacking the required vehicle path.</p>
<h4>Compare cost over the period you will own it</h4><p>1. app access on the intended phone platform; 2. required scanner or adapter hardware; 3. optional add-ons needed for the named job; 4. renewal cost during the planned ownership period; and 5. replacement cost if the interface does not fit a later vehicle.</p><details><summary>Original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><img src="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-carly/cs-052-job-first-scorecard-desktop.svg" alt="BimmerLink vs Carly: Which BMW Tool Fits Your Actual Job?: preserved source diagram 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 1; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><img src="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-carly/cs-052-app-hardware-chain-desktop.svg" alt="BimmerLink vs Carly: Which BMW Tool Fits Your Actual Job?: preserved source diagram 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 2; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article id="bimmerlink-vs-foxwell-nt530" class="legacy-case"><h3>BimmerLink vs Foxwell NT530: Phone App or BMW Handheld?</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Consolidated from the earlier `/guides/bimmerlink-vs-foxwell-nt530/` article, now redirected here. Core decision evidence and original diagrams are integrated below.</p><h4>Two different ownership models</h4><p>BimmerLink is a mobile app architecture that needs a supported interface; NT530 is a self-contained handheld sold with brand software, so the decision turns on workflow and documented vehicle/function coverage rather than a generic feature count.</p>
<h4>Decision in one paragraph</h4><p>Choose BimmerLink when a phone-based workflow, live dashboards and its documented BMW/MINI functions fit the job, and you are comfortable buying or reusing a supported interface. Choose NT530 when you want dedicated controls, a fixed cable-and-screen appliance and Foxwell's brand-software model. Neither choice should be made from “does BMW” alone.</p>
<h4>Cost belongs to the whole toolchain</h4><p>On 2026-08-29, the US iOS BimmerLink listing showed a $39.99 full-version unlock. A new buyer must add the selected adapter and tax. Someone who already owns a supported interface has a much lower incremental cost.</p><details><summary>Original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-foxwell-nt530/cs043-ownership-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-foxwell-nt530/cs043-ownership.svg" alt="BimmerLink vs Foxwell NT530: Phone App or BMW Handheld?: preserved source diagram 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 1; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-foxwell-nt530/cs043-task-boundary-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-foxwell-nt530/cs043-task-boundary.svg" alt="BimmerLink vs Foxwell NT530: Phone App or BMW Handheld?: preserved source diagram 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 2; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article id="bimmerlink-vs-protool" class="legacy-case"><h3>BimmerLink vs ProTool: Focused Diagnostics or Full Toolset?</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Consolidated from the earlier `/guides/bimmerlink-vs-protool/` article, now redirected here. Core decision evidence and original diagrams are integrated below.</p><h4>Platform is a hard gate</h4><p>If the maintained device is iOS, BimmerLink remains a candidate and ProTool does not. ProTool's publisher describes Android only. Buying another device may be rational for a wider toolset, but include that hardware and management burden in cost.</p>
<h4>Service functions require exact qualification</h4><p>BimmerLink lists functions such as battery registration, DPF regeneration, parking-brake service mode and service reset for supported cars. ProTool Diagnostic publishes tests, resets, calibrations and initialization scope. Neither list is a universal vehicle promise.</p>
<h4>Who should buy which?</h4><p>BimmerLink: owners and technicians wanting a portable focused path on iOS or Android for its documented diagnostics and service functions.</p><details><summary>Original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-protool/cs027-depth-rungs-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-protool/cs027-depth-rungs.svg" alt="BimmerLink vs ProTool: Focused Diagnostics or Full Toolset?: preserved source diagram 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 1; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-protool/cs027-platform-gate-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/bimmerlink-vs-protool/cs027-platform-gate.svg" alt="BimmerLink vs ProTool: Focused Diagnostics or Full Toolset?: preserved source diagram 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 2; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article id="ista-vs-bimmerlink" class="legacy-case"><h3>ISTA vs BimmerLink: Workshop Diagnosis or Owner App?</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Consolidated from the earlier `/guides/ista-vs-bimmerlink/` article, now redirected here. Core decision evidence and original diagrams are integrated below.</p><h4>A clean scope boundary</h4><p>Use BimmerLink for documented owner-level diagnostics and service functions when its vehicle list covers the task. Use ISTA when diagnosis depends on BMW guided test plans, workshop repair information, control-unit programming or the formal technical-information environment.</p>
<h4>What BimmerLink owns</h4><p>BimmerLink's official site describes iOS and Android support, fault-code access across control units on supported vehicles, real-time values, CSV logging and selected service operations. Examples include battery registration, diesel particulate filter functions, parking-brake service mode and service reset, with availability conditioned on the car.</p>
<h4>What ISTA adds</h4><p>BMW's Technical Information System documentation places ISTA in an independent-workshop context. Diagnosis joins vehicle identification and fault memory to guided test plans; the service supplies repair instructions, technical data and wiring information. Instead of converting a trouble code directly into a guessed part, a technician follows a vehicle-aware test path.</p><details><summary>Original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/ista-vs-bimmerlink/cs024-diagnostic-depth-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/ista-vs-bimmerlink/cs024-diagnostic-depth.svg" alt="ISTA vs BimmerLink: Workshop Diagnosis or Owner App?: preserved source diagram 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 1; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/ista-vs-bimmerlink/cs024-tool-boundary-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/ista-vs-bimmerlink/cs024-tool-boundary.svg" alt="ISTA vs BimmerLink: Workshop Diagnosis or Owner App?: preserved source diagram 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 2; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article id="protool-vs-carly" class="legacy-case"><h3>ProTool vs Carly: Which BMW App Fits Your Ownership Plan?</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Consolidated from the earlier `/guides/protool-vs-carly/` article, now redirected here. Core decision evidence and original diagrams are integrated below.</p><h4>What you buy with ProTool</h4><p>BimmerGeeks currently lists Diagnostic and Coding licenses at $99.99 each and the Master license at $174.99, checked September 1, 2026. The Master license combines the two scopes. An Android device and supported adapter remain part of the working setup unless already owned or included in a separate bundle.</p>
<h4>What you buy with Carly</h4><p>Carly's current Premium Package explanation describes a subscription license and a free universal scanner. It also says price depends on selected brand coverage, add-ons, location and currency and is shown on the website. That makes a single global dollar figure misleading.</p>
<h4>Compare three-year ownership, not just checkout</h4><p>For ProTool, total the selected license, adapter and any Android hardware. For Carly, total the displayed subscription over the period you expect to keep it, confirm renewal terms and include paid add-ons. Do not value an included adapter as a saving if it will not serve another required app.</p><details><summary>Original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/protool-vs-carly/cs-040-ownership-model-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/protool-vs-carly/cs-040-ownership-model.svg" alt="ProTool vs Carly: Which BMW App Fits Your Ownership Plan?: preserved source diagram 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 1; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/protool-vs-carly/cs-040-function-verification-flow-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/protool-vs-carly/cs-040-function-verification-flow.svg" alt="ProTool vs Carly: Which BMW App Fits Your Ownership Plan?: preserved source diagram 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 2; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article id="protool-vs-ista" class="legacy-case"><h3>ProTool vs ISTA: Which BMW Workflow Fits the Job?</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Consolidated from the earlier `/guides/protool-vs-ista/` article, now redirected here. Core decision evidence and original diagrams are integrated below.</p><h4>What ProTool covers</h4><p>BimmerGeeks describes ProTool as an Android application for BMW-family diagnostics and coding. The diagnostic license page includes fault work, live data and service functions while calling out coding exceptions such as battery and injector coding. Coding entitlement is separate unless the master license is purchased.</p>
<h4>What ISTA adds</h4><p>BMW's current Technical Information System places ISTA inside a professional technical environment. Our BMW diagnostic software for Windows guide maps the host, interface, and software-provenance checks around that environment. The official site and system-requirements PDFs describe diagnosis and programming, a qualified PC/network setup and ICOM Next as the recommended interface. BMW also documents a J2534 passthrough alternative under stated conditions.</p>
<h4>Cost is more than the software price</h4><p>ProTool's current official license prices are $99.99 Diagnostic, $99.99 Coding and $174.99 Master, checked September 1, 2026. A working total may also need an Android device and supported interface.</p><details><summary>Original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/protool-vs-ista/cs-039-capability-ladder-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/protool-vs-ista/cs-039-capability-ladder.svg" alt="ProTool vs ISTA: Which BMW Workflow Fits the Job?: preserved source diagram 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 1; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/protool-vs-ista/cs-039-environment-choice-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/protool-vs-ista/cs-039-environment-choice.svg" alt="ProTool vs ISTA: Which BMW Workflow Fits the Job?: preserved source diagram 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original comparison research diagram 2; review its evidence limits for the exact car and application.</figcaption></figure></details></article></section><style is:global>.legacy-consolidations{margin:3rem 0}.legacy-consolidations .legacy-case{border-top:1px solid var(--line,#a5b5ae);padding:1.8rem 0;scroll-margin-top:24px}.legacy-consolidations .legacy-case h3{font-size:clamp(1.35rem,2.5vw,1.8rem);line-height:1.15;margin:0 0 12px}.legacy-consolidations .legacy-case h4{font-size:1.05rem;margin:1.3rem 0 .3rem;font-weight:700}.legacy-consolidations .legacy-provenance{color:var(--muted,#52645b);font-size:13px}.legacy-consolidations details{background:#f5f7f3;border:1px solid #baccc0;border-radius:12px;padding:12px 16px;margin-top:15px}.legacy-consolidations summary{cursor:pointer;font:700 14px var(--sans,Arial)}.legacy-consolidations figure{margin:24px 0}.legacy-consolidations figure img{max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid #c3d2ca}.legacy-consolidations figcaption{font:13px/1.45 Arial,sans-serif}</style>